Title: THE RETURNS
Credit: Written by
Author: Matt Headley
Draft date: July 21, 2026
Contact: matt@gatherstudio.app

---

FADE IN:

EXT. COUNTY ROAD 12 - DAWN

Red clay. Kudzu swallowing a fence line. A truck — old, American, the kind of truck a man drives when he is done trying to impress anyone — moves slow down a two-lane road.

The road is empty. The light is the pale, uncertain light of early morning in Alabama, when the sky can't decide between pink and gray and settles on both.

A hand-painted sign passes in the truck window:

                    PLEASANT GROVE BAPTIST CHURCH
                    Sunday 10 AM
                    Rev. E. Pruitt

Below it, in newer, cheaper letters, taped with electrical tape:

                    BUILDING FOR SALE

INT. TRUCK - CONTINUOUS

CALEB DOSS (42, lean in the way that hard decades make a man lean, dressed like he's going somewhere he didn't plan to go) stares at the road. He has not shaved in three days. This is not a choice. It is an oversight.

A cardboard box sits on the passenger seat. Inside: a Bible, a stole folded badly, three ceramic coffee mugs with church logos on them, a framed photo face-down.

His hands are steady on the wheel. Everything else about him is not.

EXT. PLEASANT GROVE BAPTIST CHURCH - MOMENTS LATER

Caleb pulls off the road into a gravel lot that needs grading. The church is white clapboard, steeple intact but listing slightly. Ramp added to the front entrance, newer than the rest of the building, handrails gone to rust.

Six cars could fit in this lot. None do.

He sits in the truck. Engine off.

A crow lands on the peak of the steeple and looks at him.

EXT. PLEASANT GROVE BAPTIST CHURCH - FRONT STEPS - MOMENTS LATER

Caleb stands at the door with a key. He holds it for a moment like he is trying to remember if he wants to do this.

He opens the door.

INT. PLEASANT GROVE BAPTIST CHURCH - SANCTUARY - CONTINUOUS

Dust. Hymnals still in the pew racks — no one came back to clear them. Flower arrangement on the communion table, long dead, still standing upright. Twelve years of Sunday mornings in this smell: carpet and old wood and the particular sweetness of communion grape juice baked into the floors.

Caleb walks to the front. Stands at the pulpit.

He doesn't touch it.

From the back of the sanctuary:

RUTH (70s, small, the kind of woman who raised four children without ever raising her voice) sits in the last pew. She has been there the whole time. She is watching him with an expression that is not surprise.

RUTH
I saw your truck.

Caleb doesn't turn around.

CALEB
Mrs. Dunbar.

RUTH
You left something.

CALEB
I came for the box. From my office.

RUTH
You left something else.

Beat. He turns.

RUTH (CONT'D)
I'm not being mysterious. I mean your name is still on the sign out on 31. The big one. "Rev. Caleb Doss, Senior Pastor." Somebody ought to take it down before the new people come.

CALEB
I'll do it.

RUTH
I figured you would. That's why I'm here.

He sits in the front pew. Not the pulpit chair — the pew, same side as the congregation.

CALEB
How long has it been empty?

RUTH
Eight months. March we had the last service. Fourteen people. I brought a ham.

CALEB
Of course you did.

RUTH
Someone had to.

She moves up the aisle, slowly, one hand on each pew back as she passes. She sits two rows behind him.

RUTH (CONT'D)
Where are you living?

CALEB
My sister's.

RUTH
In Heflin?

CALEB
Yeah.

RUTH
That's not so far.

CALEB
No ma'am.

The light comes through the east windows. Dust moves in it.

RUTH
I prayed for you every day after you left.

CALEB
I know.

RUTH
I want you to know I didn't stop.

He doesn't say anything.

RUTH (CONT'D)
You don't have to say thank you. I'm not telling you so you'll say thank you.

CALEB
Why are you telling me?

RUTH
Because I think you came here to find out if anything was left. And I didn't want you to think it was empty.

A long silence.

Outside: a car on the county road. Gone.

CALEB
I wasn't a good pastor.

RUTH
You were a good pastor for eleven years. Then you were sick. Those are two different things.

CALEB
People didn't see it that way.

RUTH
Some people didn't. Harold didn't. Harold's also been married four times and blames every one of them, so I wouldn't spend much of my life worrying about Harold.

Caleb almost smiles. Almost.

CALEB
The building's going to be a furniture store. I heard.

RUTH
A consignment shop. Linda Pruett's daughter.

CALEB
That's not the worst thing.

RUTH
No. It's not.

She looks at the cross on the wall behind the pulpit. Plain wood. No corpus.

RUTH (CONT'D)
I think the building did its job. Not every building has to last forever to have done its job.

Beat.

CALEB
I'm not sure I did mine.

RUTH
Caleb.

CALEB
I'm not looking for reassurance.

RUTH
I know. I'm telling you something true. You baptized my granddaughter. You sat with Earl the whole last week. You preached the funeral for the Carver boy when nobody else would touch it because of how he died. You did your job. Then you got sick and the job got too heavy and you put it down. That's the whole story.

He looks at his hands.

CALEB
It doesn't feel like the whole story.

RUTH
It never does. That's not a flaw in the story. That's just how stories feel from the inside.

The crow is back. On the windowsill now, outside. Looking in.

CALEB
I don't know if I still believe.

She takes a moment with this.

RUTH
In what?

CALEB
Any of it. God. The whole thing.

RUTH
That's a reasonable place to be.

CALEB
You're not going to argue with me?

RUTH
Caleb. I am seventy-three years old. I have buried a husband and a son. I have sat in this building for forty years. I have had seasons where I believed and seasons where I came and sat in the pew because sitting was what I could do. I am not going to argue with a man who is being honest.

He turns and looks at her fully for the first time.

CALEB
What do you do? In the seasons you don't believe?

RUTH
I come anyway. I bring a ham. I take care of the people in front of me. I wait.

CALEB
And it comes back?

RUTH
Something comes back. I'm not sure it's exactly what left.

The dust keeps moving in the light.

RUTH (CONT'D)
You should take the sign down before you go. You'll feel better.

CALEB
I doubt it.

RUTH
You'll feel something. That's close enough.

She stands. Smooths her skirt. Walks toward the back of the sanctuary.

At the door she stops.

RUTH (CONT'D)
You know what I always liked about your preaching?

CALEB
What?

RUTH
You never pretended it was simple. Some preachers make it simple. I always thought that was a lie. You didn't lie.

She goes out.

Caleb sits in the empty church for a long moment.

He picks up one of the old hymnals. Opens it to a random page.

He reads it silently.

Sets it back.

Stands.

Picks up his cardboard box.

EXT. COUNTY ROAD 31 - LATER

Caleb stands in front of the large church sign at the highway.

REV. CALEB DOSS, SENIOR PASTOR

He brought nothing to take it down with. He pulls at the letters with his hands. They come off easier than he expected. Old adhesive.

He stands there with the letters in his hands.

He doesn't know what to do with them.

He puts them in the cardboard box, next to the Bible and the stole.

Gets in the truck.

Drives.

EXT. COUNTY ROAD 12 - MOVING

The truck goes back the way it came. Red clay. Kudzu. The pale morning light, a little stronger now.

The church sign behind him:

                    PLEASANT GROVE BAPTIST CHURCH
                    Sunday 10 AM

No name underneath.

FADE TO BLACK.

TITLE CARD: "The ruin doesn't have to last. The act of going back is what matters."

FADE OUT.
